Title: [Bug]: Raw Ethernet packet sending adds two headers leading to buffer underflow

Body:
### Bug Summary

When using any functions in NetXduo that send raw packets the STM32H5 Ethernet driver adds an extra garbage header and underflows the packet buffer.

### Detailed Description

When NetXduo sends a gPTP packet the following steps happen:
1. nx_link_ethernet_packet_send() is called
2. A header is added by the function nx_link_ethernet_header_add(). This is the correct header with the right destination and ethertype.
3. nx_link_ethernet_packet_send() calls nx_link_raw_packet_send()
4. nx_link_raw_packet_send() calls nx_stm32_eth_driver() with nx_ip_driver_command = NX_LINK_RAW_PACKET_SEND
5. nx_stm32_eth_driver() calls _nx_driver_packet_send()
6. _nx_driver_packet_send() prepends another header with incorrect destination and ethertype
7. The packet is sent by _nx_driver_hardware_packet_send()

The resulting two headers can be seen in this image:

[IMAGE ATTACHED]

- In blue are the destination addresses, note that they are different and the first one is incorrect.
- In red are the source addresses.
- In green are the ethertypes, again the first one is invalid.

To make things worse _nx_driver_packet_send() doesn't check if there is enough space to add this second header and so it underflows the packet buffer.

To fix this NX_LINK_RAW_PACKET_SEND needs to be handled separately to NX_LINK_PACKET_SEND.

Current:

C
  case NX_LINK_ARP_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_ARP_RESPONSE_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_PACKET_BROADCAST:
  case NX_LINK_RARP_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_PACKET_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_RAW_PACKET_SEND:
    {

      /* Process packet send requests.  */
      _nx_driver_packet_send(driver_req_ptr);
      break;
    }


Improved:

C
case NX_LINK_ARP_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_ARP_RESPONSE_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_PACKET_BROADCAST:
  case NX_LINK_RARP_SEND:
  case NX_LINK_PACKET_SEND:
    {

      /* Process packet send requests.  */
      _nx_driver_packet_send(driver_req_ptr);
      break;
    }


  case NX_LINK_RAW_PACKET_SEND:
    {
      /* Process raw packet send requests.  */
      _nx_driver_raw_packet_send(driver_req_ptr);
      break;
    }


And then something like:

C
static VOID  _nx_driver_raw_packet_send(NX_IP_DRIVER driver_req_ptr)
{
      /* For raw packets, the Ethernet header is already attached */
      NX_PACKETpacket_ptr = driver_req_ptr->nx_ip_driver_packet;
      UINT status;

      /* Determine if the packet exceeds the driver's MTU. */
      if (packet_ptr->nx_packet_length > NX_DRIVER_ETHERNET_MTU)
      {
          /* Indicate an unsuccessful packet send. */
          driver_req_ptr->nx_ip_driver_status = NX_DRIVER_ERROR;

          /* Link is not up or packet too big, simply free the packet. */
          nx_packet_transmit_release(packet_ptr);
          return;
      }

      /* Transmit the packet through the Ethernet controller low-level access routine. */
      status = _nx_driver_hardware_packet_send(packet_ptr);

      if (status != NX_SUCCESS)
      {
          driver_req_ptr->nx_ip_driver_status = NX_DRIVER_ERROR;
          nx_packet_transmit_release(packet_ptr);
      }
      else
      {
          driver_req_ptr->nx_ip_driver_status = NX_SUCCESS;
      }
}


### Expected Behavior

A packet is sent with one header

### Actual Behavior

A packet is sent with two headers and memory is corrupted

### Environment

TheadX, NetXduo

### Severity

Major

